--TEST--
One `Core\Secret::reveal` opens every sink `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse` refuses a `secret` at — the terminal, an interpolation, a `.` join and `Core\Json::encode` agree — ADR 0033 §§ 3-4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The agreement question: § 4's sinks each refuse a `secret` operand with their
// own diagnostic, and § 3 promises one call is the way past all of them. A sink
// that grew a second rule of its own — one that looked at the *declaration* the
// value came from rather than at its type — would still refuse here while
// looking right on its own line, which is what makes this a case and not four.
secret string $token = "hunter2";
secret bytes $key = "raw-key" as bytes;

string $revealed = Core\Secret::reveal($token, "the print-token command's whole purpose is disclosing it");
bytes $raw = Core\Secret::revealBytes($key, "the signer takes the key as bytes");

// § 4's terminal bullet, and the two shapes that spread the qualifier to a
// composed operand rather than carrying it on the binding.
echo $revealed, "\n";
echo "Bearer {$revealed}\n";
echo "Bearer " . $revealed, "\n";

// § 4's serializer bullet: `Core\Json::encode` walks the whole value, so a
// revealed one has to be accepted as the bare value and inside a container.
echo Core\Json::encode($revealed), "\n";
echo Core\Json::encode(Core\Bytes::length($raw)), "\n";
--EXPECT--
hunter2
Bearer hunter2
Bearer hunter2
"hunter2"
7
